  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 153 PM CST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nClouds have been increasing and will continue to increase this \nafternoon into tonight as our next system approaches. We did end\nup breaking the 60 degree mark again today across much of \neastern Minnesota and western Wisconsin. Looking ahead we don't \nlook likely to break 60 again until maybe a week from now when \nthe NBM 75th percentile has highs in the 60s. Overall this next \nsystem looks to follow a clipper path and typical impacts. This \nwill be rain showers generally along and north of I-94 tonight \ninto tomorrow morning based on where the forcing is expected to \nsetup for this clipper track. Behind this we will see a cold \nfrontal passage. This will bring temperatures down from the much\nabove normal start to the week. Also across western Wisconsin \nwe will see some additional precipitation chances later in the \nday tomorrow along this front, as this area will see better low \nlevel saturation. There could even, in areas like Rusk County, \nbe some snow flakes mixing in at times. With ground and air \ntemperatures near to only slightly below freezing nothing is \nexpected to stick or accumulate. Highs will vary tomorrow from \nthe 40s across central Minnesota to the lower 50s farther south \nbased on the timing of peak diurnal heating vs frontal passage. \nAs the front will be moving from north to south across the \nregion.\n\nThe airmass behind this frontal passage will bring highs down \ninto the mid 30s to lower 40s and lows into the 20s for Thursday\nand Friday. This also comes in with persistent high pressure \nwhich will keep precipitation chance low to non- existent. The \npost frontal CAA will start to fall off on Saturday allowing for\nsome warm up this weekend. Highs in the 40s appear most likely.\nWe turn to some warmer air advecting in to start the next week \nwhich should bring back highs in the 50s. With the ridge \npersisting, precipitation chances remain low.",
